Your task is to write a function `garbageCollect` that takes a reference to the root of the object graph as input and performs mark-and-sweep garbage collection. The function should traverse the object graph, mark all reachable objects, and then sweep through the memory to deallocate memory occupied by unmarked objects.

The objects in the graph are represented using the following class structure:

```swift
class Object {
    var marked: Bool = false
    var references: [Object] = []
}
```

The `Object` class has a boolean property `marked` to indicate whether the object is reachable, and an array `references` to store references to other objects it points to.

Your `garbageCollect` function should adhere to the following signature:

```swift
func garbageCollect(root: Object) {
    // Your implementation here
}
```

```swift
func garbageCollect(root: Object) {
    mark(root) // Mark all reachable objects starting from the root
    sweep()    // Sweep through the memory and deallocate unmarked objects
}

func mark(_ obj: Object) {
    if obj.marked {
        return
    }
    obj.marked = true
    for reference in obj.references {
        mark(reference) // Recursively mark referenced objects
    }
}

func sweep() {
    // Iterate through all allocated memory and deallocate unmarked objects
    for obj in allocatedMemory {
        if !obj.marked {
            deallocate(obj)
        } else {
            obj.marked = false // Reset the marked flag for the next garbage collection cycle
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `garbageCollect` function initiates the mark-and-sweep process by first marking all reachable objects starting from the root, and then sweeping through the memory to deallocate unmarked objects. The `mark` function recursively marks all referenced objects, and the `sweep` function iterates through the allocated memory to deallocate unmarked objects.

