struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* left;
    Node* right;
};

Node* findLowestCommonAncestor(Node* root, Node* node1, Node* node2) {
    if (root == NULL || node1 == NULL || node2 == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    if (root == node1 || root == node2) {
        return root;
    }

    if (node1->data < root->data && node2->data < root->data) {
        return findLowestCommonAncestor(root->left, node1, node2);
    }

    if (node1->data > root->data && node2->data > root->data) {
        return findLowestCommonAncestor(root->right, node1, node2);
    }

    return root;
}
```

To optimize the algorithm for a balanced binary search tree, we can take advantage of the property that the left subtree contains values less than the root, and the right subtree contains values greater than the root. By comparing the values of the nodes with the root, we can determine which subtree to search in.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n) in a balanced binary search tree, where n is the number of nodes in the tree. This is because in each recursive call, the number of remaining nodes to search is halved.

