The principal difference between a linked list and a dynamic array is the way they store and access elements. 

In a linked list, elements are stored as individual nodes, where each node contains a value and a reference to the next node. This allows for dynamic allocation of memory as each node can be stored anywhere in memory. However, accessing elements in a linked list has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the list. This is because in order to access an element, the list needs to be traversed from the beginning until the desired element is found.

On the other hand, a dynamic array is a contiguous block of memory that can be resized as needed. Elements in a dynamic array can be accessed using the index operator, which has a time complexity of O(1). This is because elements in a dynamic array are stored next to each other in memory, allowing for direct access using an index.

The space complexity for both data structures is O(n), as they both require memory to store the elements.

