std::vector<int> customSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
    return arr;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> unsorted_arr = {45, 21, 12, 56, 7, 47, 34};

    std::vector<int> sorted_arr = customSort(unsorted_arr);

    std::cout << "Sorted array: ";
    for (int num : sorted_arr) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Explanation:
The customSort function implements a basic Bubble Sort algorithm. It iterates over the array multiple times, swapping adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order. This process is repeated until the entire array is sorted.

Since we are not using any additional data structures or recursion, the space complexity is O(1).

The time complexity of this sorting algorithm is O(n^2) in the average case, as it has nested loops that iterate over the array. However, in the worst case, where the array is already sorted in descending order, the time complexity would be O(n^2) as well. Therefore, the algorithm is stable in both the average and worst case scenarios.

This implementation can handle arrays of size up to 10^6 elements and elements with values up to 10^9, as per the given constraints.

