    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}

A self-balancing binary search tree, such as an AVL tree or a Red-Black tree, is a binary search tree that automatically keeps its height balanced. This means that the heights of the left and right subtrees of every node differ by at most one, ensuring efficient search, insert, and delete operations with a worst-case time complexity of O(log n). In a regular binary search tree, if the elements are inserted in sorted order, the tree becomes linear, leading to a worst-case time complexity of O(n) for search, insert, and delete operations.

A callback function, also known as a function pointer, is a function that is passed as an argument to another function and is called within that function. The callback function can be used to customize the behavior of the function that receives it. In the binary search tree program, we can use a function pointer as a callback function to perform a custom action during a specific operation, such as printing the value of each node during an in-order traversal.

Example of using a function pointer as a callback function in the binary search tree program:

// Function pointer type alias for callback function
typedef void (*CallbackFunction)(Node*);

// Function to perform in-order traversal of the binary search tree with a callback function
void inOrderTraversalWithCallback(Node* root, CallbackFunction callback) {
    if (root != NULL) {
        inOrderTraversalWithCallback(root->left, callback);
        callback(root);
        inOrderTraversalWithCallback(root->right, callback);
    }
}

// Callback function to print the value of each node during in-order traversal
void printNodeValue(Node* node) {
    printf("%d ", node->data);
}

int main() {
    Node* root = NULL;

    // Insert nodes into the binary search tree

    // ...

    // Print the binary search tree using in-order traversal with callback function
    printf("In-order traversal of the binary search tree with callback function: ");
    inOrderTraversalWithCallback(root, printNodeValue);
    printf("\n");

    // ...

    return 0;
}

In this example, the `inOrderTraversalWithCallback` function takes a `CallbackFunction` as an argument. During the in-order traversal, for each node, the callback function is called and the value of the node is printed. The `printNodeValue` function serves as the callback function in this case. By using function pointers as callback functions, we can easily modify the behavior of the traversal function without having to modify the function itself.

