Here's a recursive function in PHP to check if a number is prime, without using any built-in functions:

```php
function isPrime($n)
{
    // Handling edge cases
    if (!is_int($n) || $n <= 1) {
        return false;
    }

    // Recursive base case
    if ($n == 2) {
        return true;
    }

    // Check if the number is divisible by any smaller odd number
    if ($n % 2 == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    return isPrimeRecursive($n, 3); // Call the recursive helper function
}

function isPrimeRecursive($n, $divisor)
{
    if ($divisor * $divisor > $n) {
        return true;
    }

    if ($n % $divisor == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    return isPrimeRecursive($n, $divisor + 2);
}
```

In this code, `isPrime()` is the main function that checks if a number is prime. It handles edge cases and then calls the recursive helper function `isPrimeRecursive()` to perform the actual prime checking.

The `isPrimeRecursive()` function uses a divisor starting from 3 and iterates up to the square root of the number being checked. It checks if the number is divisible by any smaller odd number, and if so, returns false. Otherwise, it recursively calls itself with the next odd divisor.

