To achieve a space complexity of O(n), we can use a data structure, such as a HashMap or an ArrayList of ArrayLists, to store the indices of duplicate elements. For each element in the array, we will check if it exists in the data structure. If it does, we will add the current index to the list of indices for that element. If it doesn't exist, we will create a new entry in the data structure with the current element as the key and a list containing the current index as the value.

Here is a sample implementation in Java:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class DuplicateElements {
    public static Map<Integer, List<Integer>> findDuplicateIndices(int[] arr) {
        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> duplicateIndices = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            int element = arr[i];

            if (duplicateIndices.containsKey(element)) {
                duplicateIndices.get(element).add(i);
            } else {
                List<Integer> indices = new ArrayList<>();
                indices.add(i);
                duplicateIndices.put(element, indices);
            }
        }

        return duplicateIndices;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5, 4};
        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> duplicateIndices = findDuplicateIndices(arr);

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, List<Integer>> entry : duplicateIndices.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println("Element " + entry.getKey() + " has duplicate indices: " + entry.getValue());
        }
    }
}
```

The time complexity of this solution is O(n^2) because we are using a nested loop to compare each pair of elements. The outer loop iterates n times, and the inner loop iterates n-1, n-2, n-3, ..., 1 times, resulting in a total of n(n-1)/2 comparisons. In big O notation, this is simplified to O(n^2).

The space complexity of this solution is O(n) because the maximum number of entries in the HashMap is equal to the number of unique elements in the input array, which can be at most n. Each entry in the HashMap contains a list of indices, which can have a maximum length of n as well. Therefore, the overall space complexity is O(n).

