To initialize a 2D array in C where the elements in each row are set to the sum of the row number and column number, you can use a nested loop. However, achieving a time complexity of O(n^2) and space complexity of O(1) requires some clever techniques.

Here's the code that fulfills the given requirements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void initializeArray(int arr[][3], int n) {
    int sum = 0;

    // Fill the first row with sum values
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        sum += j;
        arr[0][j] = sum;
    }

    // Copy the values from the first row to the remaining rows
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            arr[i][j] = arr[0][j] + i;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    int n = 3;
    int arr[n][n];

    initializeArray(arr, n);

    // Printing the initialized 2D array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            printf("%d ", arr[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we use the `initializeArray` function to initialize the 2D array `arr` of size `n`x`n`. The first loop fills the first row with the sum values, and the second loop copies the values from the first row to the remaining rows. Finally, we print the initialized array.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n^2) because we iterate through each element of the 2D array once. The space complexity is O(1) because we only use a fixed-size 2D array `arr` and a few additional variables (`sum`, `i`, `j`).

