Here is a C program that prints the Fibonacci series up to the nth number, but only if the nth number satisfies the given conditions:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Function to check if a number is prime
int isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
        return 0;
    }

    for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
        if (num % i == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
    }

    return 1;
}

// Function to calculate the sum of digits of a number
int digitSum(int num) {
    int sum = 0;

    while (num != 0) {
        sum += num % 10;
        num /= 10;
    }

    return sum;
}

// Function to count the number of distinct prime factors of a number
int countPrimeFactors(int num) {
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
        if (num % i == 0) {
            while (num % i == 0) {
                num /= i;
            }
            count++;
        }
    }

    if (num > 1) {
        count++;
    }

    return count;
}

// Function to print the Fibonacci series up to the nth number,
// which satisfies the given conditions
void printFibonacci(int n) {
    int a = 0, b = 1, c;
    int count = 0;

    printf("Fibonacci series up to the %dth number satisfying the conditions:\n", n);

    while (count < n) {
        c = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = c;

        if (isPrime(b) && digitSum(b) % 3 == 0 && isPrime(countPrimeFactors(b))) {
            printf("%d ", b);
            count++;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    int n;

    printf("Enter the value of n: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    printFibonacci(n);

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we have defined three helper functions `isPrime`, `digitSum`, and `countPrimeFactors`. These functions are used to check if a number is prime, calculate the sum of digits of a number, and count the number of distinct prime factors of a number, respectively.

The `printFibonacci` function takes an integer `n` as input and prints the Fibonacci series up to the nth number, which satisfies the given conditions. It uses the variables `a`, `b`, and `c` to store the Fibonacci sequence. The variable `count` keeps track of the number of Fibonacci numbers printed. The loop continues until `count` reaches `n`. For each Fibonacci number, we check if it is prime, has a digit sum divisible by 3, and the number of distinct prime factors is a prime number itself. If all conditions are satisfied, the number is printed.

In the `main` function, we take the value of `n` as input from the user and call the `printFibonacci` function.

Note: The program assumes that the input `n` is a positive integer.

