To remove the extra spaces from a string, we can iterate through the string character by character. If we encounter a space, we check the previous character. If the previous character is also a space, we skip it. Otherwise, we add the space to the result string. If the current character is not a space, we add it to the result string.

To remove duplicate characters, we can use a set to keep track of the characters we have seen so far. If we encounter a character that is already in the set, we skip it. Otherwise, we add it to the set and add it to the result string.

Here is the implementation of the `removeExtraSpaces` function in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_set>
using namespace std;

string removeExtraSpaces(string s) {
    string result;
    unordered_set<char> seen;
    char prev = '\0'; // initialize prev as null character

    for (char c : s) {
        if (c == ' ') {
            if (prev != ' ') {
                result += c;
            }
        } else {
            if (seen.find(c) == seen.end()) {
                seen.insert(c);
                result += c;
            }
        }
        prev = c;
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    string s = "   hello  world   ";
    string result = removeExtraSpaces(s);
    cout << result << endl; // should print "helo wrd"

    return 0;
}
