```java
public static int retrievePrimeIndex(int[] arr, int index) {
    if (isPrime(index)) {
        return arr[index];
    } else {
        return retrievePrimeIndex(arr, index + 1);
    }
}

public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
    if (number <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    return isPrimeHelper(number, 2);
}

public static boolean isPrimeHelper(int number, int divisor) {
    if (divisor == number) {
        return true;
    } else if (number % divisor == 0) {
        return false;
    } else {
        return isPrimeHelper(number, divisor + 1);
    }
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] arr = {11, 30, 45, 19, 20, 60};
    int primeIndex = 2; // Assuming the first prime index is 2 (as 0 and 1 are not prime)
    int result = retrievePrimeIndex(arr, primeIndex);
    System.out.println("Item at prime index " + primeIndex + ": " + result);
}
```

In this solution, we define a recursive function `retrievePrimeIndex` that takes the array and the current index as parameters. If the current index is a prime number (determined by the `isPrime` function), we return the item at that index from the array. Otherwise, we recursively call `retrievePrimeIndex` with the next index (index + 1) until we find a prime index.

