Here is an example of a Java program that checks if a given string is a palindrome, while ignoring non-alphanumeric characters and following the time and space complexity requirements:

```java
public class PalindromeChecker {

    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        // Convert the string to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison
        s = s.toLowerCase();

        int i = 0;
        int j = s.length() - 1;

        while (i < j) {
            // Ignore non-alphanumeric characters
            while (i < j && !isAlphanumeric(s.charAt(i))) {
                i++;
            }
            while (i < j && !isAlphanumeric(s.charAt(j))) {
                j--;
            }

            if (s.charAt(i) != s.charAt(j)) {
                return false;
            }

            i++;
            j--;
        }

        return true;
    }

    private static boolean isAlphanumeric(char c) {
        return Character.isLetter(c) || Character.isDigit(c);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "A man, a plan, a canal, Panama!";
        System.out.println(isPalindrome(s)); // Output: true

        s = "race car";
        System.out.println(isPalindrome(s)); // Output: true

        s = "hello world";
        System.out.println(isPalindrome(s)); // Output: false
    }
}
```

In this program, the `isPalindrome` method uses two pointers (`i` and `j`) to check if the characters at the corresponding positions in the string are equal, while ignoring non-alphanumeric characters. The method `isAlphanumeric` checks if a character is a letter or a digit. The main method demonstrates the usage of the `isPalindrome` method with some example strings.

