Here's a recursive function in Java that calculates the Fibonacci numbers up to the number 10000, with a time complexity of O(2^n):

```java
public class FibonacciCalculator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int n = 10;
            long[] fibonacciSequence = calculateFibonacciSequence(n);
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(fibonacciSequence));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public static long[] calculateFibonacciSequence(int n) throws Exception {
        if (n < 0 || n > 10000) {
            throw new Exception("Invalid input: input number should be between 0 and 10000");
        }

        long[] fibonacciSequence = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            fibonacciSequence[i] = calculateFibonacciNumber(i);
        }

        return fibonacciSequence;
    }

    private static long calculateFibonacciNumber(int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        } else if (n == 1) {
            return 1;
        } else {
            return calculateFibonacciNumber(n - 1) + calculateFibonacciNumber(n - 2);
        }
    }
}
```

This code calculates the Fibonacci numbers up to the input number `n` (in this case, `n = 10`). It first checks if the input number is valid (between 0 and 10000) and throws an exception if it's not. Then, it initializes an array `fibonacciSequence` with a length of `n + 1`. It then iterates from 0 to `n` and calculates each Fibonacci number using the `calculateFibonacciNumber` function. Finally, it returns the `fibonacciSequence` array.

Note: The time complexity of this recursive approach is O(2^n) because each Fibonacci number is calculated by recursively calculating the two previous Fibonacci numbers.

