To implement the hashmap with separate chaining, you can use the `HashMap` class provided by Java's `util` package. Here's an example code that fulfills the requirements:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class CustomHashMap<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final LinkedList<Entry<K, V>>[] buckets;
    private int size;

    public CustomHashMap(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.buckets = new LinkedList[capacity];
        this.size = 0;
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        int index = getIndex(key);
        Entry<K, V> entry = new Entry<>(key, value);

        if (buckets[index] == null) {
            buckets[index] = new LinkedList<>();
        }

        LinkedList<Entry<K, V>> bucket = buckets[index];
        for (Entry<K, V> existingEntry : bucket) {
            if (existingEntry.getKey().equals(key)) {
                existingEntry.setValue(value);
                return;
            }
        }

        bucket.add(entry);
        size++;

        if ((double) size / capacity >= 0.75) {
            resize();
        }
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        int index = getIndex(key);
        LinkedList<Entry<K, V>> bucket = buckets[index];

        if (bucket != null) {
            for (Entry<K, V> entry : bucket) {
                if (entry.getKey().equals(key)) {
                    return entry.getValue();
                }
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    public void remove(K key) {
        int index = getIndex(key);
        LinkedList<Entry<K, V>> bucket = buckets[index];

        if (bucket != null) {
            for (Entry<K, V> entry : bucket) {
                if (entry.getKey().equals(key)) {
                    bucket.remove(entry);
                    size--;
                    return;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private int getIndex(K key) {
        return Math.abs(key.hashCode() % capacity);
    }

    private void resize() {
        int newCapacity = capacity * 2;
        LinkedList<Entry<K, V>>[] newBuckets = new LinkedList[newCapacity];

        for (LinkedList<Entry<K, V>> bucket : buckets) {
            if (bucket != null) {
                for (Entry<K, V> entry : bucket) {
                    int newIndex = Math.abs(entry.getKey().hashCode() % newCapacity);
                    if (newBuckets[newIndex] == null) {
                        newBuckets[newIndex] = new LinkedList<>();
                    }
                    newBuckets[newIndex].add(entry);
                }
            }
        }

        buckets = newBuckets;
        capacity = newCapacity;
    }

    private static class Entry<K, V> {
        private final K key;
        private V value;

        public Entry(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }

        public K getKey() {
            return key;
        }

        public V getValue() {
            return value;
        }

        public void setValue(V value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
}
```

Here's an example of how you can use this `CustomHashMap` class:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CustomHashMap<String, String> map = new CustomHashMap<>(10);

        map.put("Car", "Honda");
        map.put("Flower", "Rose");

        System.out.println(map.get("Car")); // Output: Honda
        System.out.println(map.get("Flower")); // Output: Rose

        map.remove("Car");

        System.out.println(map.get("Car")); // Output: null
    }
}
```

This implementation uses separate chaining to handle collisions by storing collided elements in separate linked lists. The `getIndex()` method calculates the bucket index for a given key by taking the absolute value of the key's hash code modulo the capacity.

To implement open addressing, you can modify the `CustomHashMap` class to use an array of entries directly instead of linked lists. You can use linear probing to find alternative indices for collided elements.

To maintain the O(1) time complexity for all operations, you need to ensure that the load factor (number of elements / capacity) does not exceed a certain threshold. If it does, you can resize the hashmap by doubling the capacity and rehashing all the elements.

